WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:10.620
Here’s something weird: A conversation about balance in League of Legends changed the pro scene forever.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:15.740
In 2016, pro players and team owners were mad that Riot Games kept changing their game

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.460
at the worst possible times.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.140
And in their attempt to pressure Riot to re-think the way they patch their game around pro play,

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.660
they ended up changing the business model of league esports scene forever.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.600
And the craziest part about that, and that we are responsible for it.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:30.800
Sort of.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:32.940
Kind of.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.120
"No. No."

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:34.760
Maybe?

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:35.900
"Hell no."

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:36.720
A little.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:37.220
"No."

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.660
2016 was a frustrating time for League pro players and team owners.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.140
It’s not that the scene was stagnating, far from it, it was actually growing faster than ever

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:58.609
But there weren't a lot of people outside of Riot who were seeing the benefits of that growth.

00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:02.800
See, League at that point was still a pretty risky venture.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.050
Sure, it was one of the biggest esports in the world, but it was a pretty uncertain proposition

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.900
for a non endemic investor looking to get into the space.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.700
Teams were still able to be relegated, and if you happened to put together a team that

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.280
wasn’t, say, good enough for the current meta, your investment went down the drain at the

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.300
end of the season.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:29.060
(Casting)

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.620
And you didn’t even have to put together a terrible team to be at risk.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:37.120
See winning is popularity, and if you had a team that was good enough to stave off relegation,

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.820
but not good enough to win most of their games, you weren't going to build up the kind of fanbase

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.280
that sponsors are looking for.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:48.080
Naturally it was a hot topic going into the 2016 NA LCS Summer Finals.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.720
So in an interview with Team SoloMid owner Andy "Reginald" Dinh, ex-pro Scarra asked him about it.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.220
"Like what's your personal opinion on these types of last minute changes."

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.360
W-w-w-wait, can we get a zoom-in on that mic flag?

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.540
That's right. Scarra was asking that question for theScore esports.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.340
Which if I'm being intellectually honest, means nothing, doesn't matter, and has nothing to do with the rest of

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:08.980
this story.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:15.660
But, if I'm being petty? Which I will be. It means that that seemingly innocuous question is responsible for the

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.440
sh*tstorm that you're gonna hear about in the rest of this video.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:19.400
Back to the interview.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.340
"Well I mean, if you look at it from a fan perspective right they have a lot of fun

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:30.780
watching LCS and Worlds with new champions. It just gets all crazy seeing a bunch of new champions.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.200
seeing a Darius and GP running around just killing everyone.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.340
But like, from an owner perspective and a player perspective, it's honestly really discouraging.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:44.180
In order to make like an easy example, I would probably say it's like... If you look at the NBA right, where they go

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:50.480
into NBA playoffs, it would be essentially changing the basketball's weight, and changing into like shooting a

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:56.220
bowling ball instead of a basketball. And a lot of my players, practice like 10 to 12 hours a day.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:02.280
And it's not fun practice either 'cause they wake up at like 10. They do VOD review 10-12 and they scrim

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:08.200
from 12 to 9pm and then they play solo queue. They're pretty much really invested into this.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.600
And all their hard work essentially goes away."

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.180
But Regi went a step further.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.440
He pointed out that the constant meta shifts make it really difficult for a non-endemic investor to feel a team.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.220
Particularly because they don't really understand the game.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:26.460
"From a lower organization standpoint, a lot of the investors are buying into esports

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.500
because they don't understand how to navigate around. They don't understand what to do.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.380
It's a lot harder for them, so they're losing millions of dollars by putting money into

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.940
something they don't understand.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:40.220
That's with any investment right? If you don't understand a space and you put money into it,

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.840
then you're basically just taking a gamble."

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:47.320
And if the game is constantly changing with relegation as the punishment for getting the meta wrong,

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.020
That gamble is just too risky.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:55.420
Needless to say Riot didn't love getting called out like this by one of their more prominent team owners.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.920
See, Team SoloMid is the most popular team in North America.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.820
And if Regi was railing on Riot. TSM fans were likely to follow.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.960
But Riot didn't respond with a carefully worded press release.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:12.520
In fact they probably wouldn't have said anything and handled everything behind closed doors.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:18.520
However Riot co-founder Marc "Tryndamere" Merrill seemed to take Regi's comments personally.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:23.960
In the original comment he posted to the League of Legends reddit, Merrill wrote, "Love me some Regi.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:29.360
But if he's so concerned about the financial health of his players, maybe he should spend some more of the

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:35.120
millions he has made/makes from League of Legends on paying them instead of investing in other esports

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.520
where he is losing money?"

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.620
Merrill went on to criticize more of Regi's comments. Including saying that team owners did have the means

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:44.280
to pay players better, they were just holding out on them.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:49.200
And that patches were being designed to make the viewing experience more exciting.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.760
Not to balance the game better for pro play.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.480
As you might expect, that didn't go over great.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.080
Even though Merrill edited his post to tone down the language.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:04.540
His glib, seemingly uninformed, and mostly unpopular comments spread across the League of Legends

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.700
community like mimetic wildfire.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:12.600
And Regi was quick to respond. The day after Merrill's reddit comments he released a Twitlonger in which he

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.420
pretty thoroughly rebuked most of Merrill's points.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:22.260
In particular, he had this rather scathing response to Merrill's assertation that Regi shouldn't be spending

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.500
the money he makes on League of Legends into other games.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:31.680
"Most LCS teams lose money. Because stipends are stagnant, sponsorships for LCS operations are

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.320
shrinking and the cost of player salaries, content production, support staff and housing costs

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:37.660
are spiraling up."

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:42.380
Regi pointed out that sponsorship activation opportunities were scarce.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:48.340
And that Riot's team stipends didn't even come close to covering the cost of operations for an LCS team.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:53.720
And while teams had fewer ways to make money than ever, players' salaries were naturally going up.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:58.400
As great players realized they could ask for more money from teams that wanted to win.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:04.220
And again, outside investors just weren't interested in a system that could see them lose everything to just

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:05.800
one bad split.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.840
Tryndamere came back with a twitlonger of his own.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:13.540
Effectively walking back a lot of his claims and agreeing with most of Regi's points.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.860
But the damage was done.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:19.920
Public opinion had been pulled out from under Riot. And the time was right to instigate change.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:26.120
Reginald made one more Twitlonger post on August 24, telling Merrill that a proposal from several NALCS

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:32.620
team owners and players would be in his inbox that day, ending the post with a hashtag. #LCSForever.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:38.860
#LCSForever spread quickly too. With several NALCS team owners banding together under its banner.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:43.540
And it seemed like for the first time in a while, they were going to the negotiation table with some form of

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:44.820
leverage over Riot.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:48.180
But fans wouldn't get to find out exactly what that meant for a while.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:53.040
It took until November for firm reporting to come out about Riot's negotiations with teams.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.620
When Slingshot Esports published a letter from teams to Riot detailing some of their demands.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:03.440
Among other things, the documents outlined some of the team owners proposals and responses to Riot's

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:04.000
proposals.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.080
And by April 2017, we at theScore esports were reporting that Riot was getting ready to franchise

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.480
for the 2018 season.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:15.260
Finally, in June, 10 months after Regi's initial interview with Scarra.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:17.920
Riot announced franchising was coming to the NA LCS.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:22.060
With no relegation, permanent spots for teams and revenue sharing.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:27.020
"And we think this is great because for teams this will enable them to make better investments and more

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:28.200
investments into their squad.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:32.420
And it should in theory help with competitive parity it gives revenues up and down the table.

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.100
And for pros. We think this is a big change.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:39.460
Because it gives them upside and benefit from the growth of the league."

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:44.740
In the end, Regi's comments very directly led to Riot implementing a franchise system that has only been

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:48.260
healthy for the scene, and has helped all of esports too.

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:53.940
We saw franchising being discussed as a serious option for even more games' leagues and more non-endemics

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:55.540
putting their money into the scene.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:58.240
Unfortunately it wasn't all roses and sunshine.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.160
EU got kinda screwed while NA flourished under franchising.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:08.820
In fact, Europe isn't getting franchising until 2019 after split after split of declining viewership,

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.120
revenue and players leaving for NA.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.160
But franchising did happen.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.340
And it was thanks, in part, to us.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.420
"Where do you get off?"

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:22.420
Totally us you guys, definitely not that Riot was probably considering franchising

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:27.580
beforehand and that they only acted after a concerted effort from team owners and pressure from

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:29.580
fans in order to make the scene better and

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:35.300
even then only after nearly a year of negotiations... It was definitely us. Mission accomplished.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:37.060
You're welcome?

